    <h1>How to Read an HVAC Quote</h1>
    <p class="intro-lead">
      Two HVAC contractors can price the exact same 3-ton heat pump within $400 of each other while offering completely different scopes of work. One quote covers city mechanical permits, a fresh copper line set, and proper refrigerant recovery; the other is a three-line total that triggers $1,200 in change orders once your old condenser is sitting in the driveway.
    </p>

    <h2>What a Complete Replacement Quote Should List</h2>
    <p>
      A fair, auditable installation bid reads like a parts-and-labor manifest rather than a brochure. Before comparing prices across contractors, verify that every estimate spells out these six core line items:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Exact outdoor and indoor model numbers:</strong> You need the manufacturer, tonnage, and AHRI Certified Reference Number so you can confirm the matched system actually meets U.S. Department of Energy (DOE) regional minimums (13.4 SEER2 in the North, or 14.3 SEER2 / 13.8 SEER2 in the Southeast and Southwest under 10 CFR 430.32).</li>
      <li><strong>Refrigerant type specified on the bid:</strong> Under the EPA AIM Act phasedown, new residential air conditioners and heat pumps sold today transition from R-410A to lower-GWP A2L refrigerants such as R-454B or R-32. Your quote should state which refrigerant the matched coil and condenser use.</li>
      <li><strong>Labor scope and installation materials:</strong> Look for condenser pad leveling, condensate drain trap and float safety switch, electrical disconnect box and whip, and sheet-metal plenum transitions.</li>
      <li><strong>Repair vs. replacement warranty terms separated clearly:</strong> Manufacturer parts warranties (typically 10 years when registered) cover defective components, not refrigerant or labor. A trustworthy quote separates the manufacturer parts warranty from the contractor’s labor warranty in years.</li>
      <li><strong>Load calculation method:</strong> A written note stating that equipment capacity is verified via an ACCA Manual J load calculation rather than guessing from square footage.</li>
      <li><strong>Payment schedule and cancellation terms:</strong> Standard milestones split deposits, delivery/install completion, and final municipal inspection pass rather than demanding 100% upfront.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Five Items Most Commonly Missing From HVAC Quotes</h2>
    <p>
      When a bid comes in suspiciously lower than two competing quotes, check the five items below first. Leaving them out does not eliminate the work—it only shifts the cost to an invoice surprise later.
    </p>

    <div tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Commonly omitted HVAC quote line items" class="table-wrapper">
      <table class="estimate-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Omitted Line Item</th>
            <th>Why It Matters Technically</th>
            <th>What to Demand in Writing</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Municipal Permits &amp; Inspection</th>
            <td>Most US cities require a mechanical permit (and electrical permit if breaker size changes). Unpermitted work creates insurance and home-sale disclosure headaches.</td>
            <td>“Contractor pulls and pays for city mechanical/electrical permits and schedules final inspection.”</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">EPA 608 Recovery &amp; Disposal</th>
            <td>Under EPA Section 608 rules, venting refrigerant to the atmosphere is illegal. Old equipment and refrigerant must be recovered and hauled away properly.</td>
            <td>“Includes EPA Section 608 recovery of existing refrigerant and haul-away/disposal of old units.”</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Copper Line Set Replacement</th>
            <td>Moving from an R-22 system (mineral oil) to R-410A, R-454B, or R-32 (POE synthetic oil) leaves incompatible oil residue and undersized suction lines if reused carelessly.</td>
            <td>“New insulated copper suction and liquid line set sized to manufacturer spec (or pressure-tested flush protocol if inaccessible).”</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Thermostat &amp; Low-Voltage Wire</th>
            <td>Two-stage or heat-pump systems often require extra thermostat conductors (5 to 8 wires) or an outdoor sensor that old 4-wire cables lack.</td>
            <td>“Thermostat model included; replace or add low-voltage thermostat cable if existing conductors are insufficient.”</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Ductwork &amp; Plenum Transitions</th>
            <td>New air handlers rarely match 18-year-old cabinet dimensions. Forcing a new cabinet onto leaking or undersized return drops static pressure and kills blowers early.</td>
            <td>“Custom sheet-metal supply/return plenum transitions, mastic sealing, and filter rack replacement included.”</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Worked Example: Comparing Two Quotes Side by Side</h2>
    <div class="worked-example">
      <p>
        Suppose a homeowner with a 15-year-old R-22 3-ton split AC receives two quotes to replace the system:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Contractor A (“Quick Bid”):</strong> Total written price <strong>$7,900</strong>. Text says “Install 3-ton 14 SEER2 AC and coil, standard installation.” No model numbers, no line-set mention, and small print at the bottom says “Permits and code upgrades billed separately if required.”</li>
        <li><strong>Contractor B (“Itemized Bid”):</strong> Total written price <strong>$9,400</strong>. Lists matched condenser and evaporator coil AHRI reference number rated at 15.2 SEER2, specifies new insulated copper line set, new 240V whip and fused disconnect, sheet-metal return box with 4-inch media filter cabinet, condensate float switch, EPA 608 recovery/disposal, municipal mechanical permit, and a 2-year labor warranty.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
        <strong>The math check:</strong> Adding a missed municipal permit ($250), a new line set ($750), a disconnect/drain safety kit ($300), and a media filter transition ($400) turns Contractor A’s “$7,900” bid into <strong>$9,600</strong>—with zero clarity on equipment matching or warranty. Contractor B’s $9,400 itemized quote is actually cheaper and carries verifiable DOE compliance.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Red Flags on Repair Quotes Too</h2>
    <p>
      Vague quotes are just as dangerous on a $900 repair call as on a $9,400 replacement:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>No diagnostic breakdown:</strong> A repair quote that says “Fix AC — $1,150” without naming the failed part (capacitor, contactor, blower motor, TXV valve, or refrigerant leak location) makes it impossible to check whether the tech is guessing.</li>
      <li><strong>“Top off refrigerant” without finding the leak:</strong> Refrigerant is not consumed like fuel. If your system is low, adding R-22 or R-410A without locating and repairing the leak guarantees you pay twice.</li>
      <li><strong>Same-day expiration discounts:</strong> High-pressure claims that a replacement price jumps $1,500 if you do not sign before the technician leaves your driveway are sales tactics, not technical realities.</li>
      <li><strong>Skipping the model/serial nameplate:</strong> If a tech quotes an R-22 recharge or coil swap without inspecting the outdoor unit’s refrigerant stamp and age, pause and ask for a written scope.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Next Step: Put Your Quote Through the Numbers</h2>
    <p>
      Once you have confirmed the repair and replacement quotes are genuinely apples-to-apples, plug both numbers into our <a href="/">HVAC Repair or Replace Calculator</a> to see whether the $5,000 rule, 50% rule, R-22 check, or your stay horizon leans repair or replace. If you are replacing, read <a href="/guides/how-to-vet-hvac-contractor/">how to vet an HVAC contractor</a> before picking the winning bid.
    </p>
